# BUG-215 regression fixture — `str.indexOf` takes ONE argument.
#
# The offset form is a separately named method, `indexOfFrom(sub, from)`, which
# returns `int?`.  Writing `indexOf(sub, from)` used to SILENTLY drop the offset:
# every search restarted at 0, so a scan loop got the same index forever and then
# panicked inside the `substring(a, b)` that consumed it with a > b.  That is
# exactly how `IDE/ZebraIDE.zbr` crashed on the Check and List Targets buttons.
#
# The first fix put a `@compileError` in CODEGEN, so this fixture emitted
# successfully and the marker had to be found in the generated Zig. #5a replaced
# that with a real front-end check (TypeChecker.stdlibArityError, driven by the
# verified table in tools/stdlib_arity.tsv), so the call is now rejected BEFORE
# emit, with a Zebra diagnostic at the user's own line and column.
#
# That is the outcome BUG-215 wanted: the error stopped living inside generated
# code. It also generalises — the codegen guard covered `indexOf` alone, while the
# table covers the whole documented surface in both directions (BUG-222 is the
# too-FEW-arguments half). Asserted by `smoke_tc_fail`, matching BUG-218's fixture.

def main()
    var s = "a:b:c"
    var i = s.indexOf(":", 2)
    print(i.toString())
